WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
LightGBM.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.563
On big tabular data, it is usually the fastest strong option.

3
00:00:06.563 --> 00:00:08.100
Two ideas explain why.

4
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.600
The first idea is histogram binning.

5
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.600
Continuous features start as raw values.

6
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.967
LightGBM pre-buckets them into about two hundred fifty five bins, once.

7
00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:24.833
Finding the best split now means scanning bins, instead of sorting raw values.

8
00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:28.367
The second idea is how the tree grows.

9
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:33.995
Level-wise growth expands every level evenly, as XGBoost classically did.

10
00:00:33.995 --> 00:00:38.667
LightGBM always splits the leaf with the biggest loss reduction.

11
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:41.067
That makes deeper, lopsided trees.

12
00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:45.633
Which spend each split where it helps the most, so you get more accuracy per split.

13
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:48.500
Leaf-wise simply chases the biggest wins.

14
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.267
Here is what that looks like.

15
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:53.000
The root splits into two.

16
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:58.970
Only the branch that reduces loss the most keeps growing, while the other stays a single leaf.

17
00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.167
The result is deep on one side, shallow on the other.

18
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:04.433
Let's race them.

19
00:01:04.733 --> 00:01:06.433
Both get five hundred trees.

20
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:07.933
We time each fit.

21
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:12.305
Then check the AUC, to confirm accuracy is about equal.

22
00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:15.267
LightGBM is often several times quicker.

23
00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:17.967
Here is why speed matters.

24
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:27.333
Scale the exoplanet classifier from five thousand confirmed planets to two hundred thousand Kepler objects of interest, with forty features each.

25
00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:30.648
XGBoost takes minutes per experiment.

26
00:01:30.648 --> 00:01:34.967
LightGBM's bins cut that to seconds, at the same AUC.

27
00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:38.968
So you test five feature ideas an hour instead of one.

28
00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:41.367
Speed becomes accuracy, indirectly.

29
00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:43.672
There is a trade.

30
00:01:43.672 --> 00:01:46.467
Leaf-wise trees can overfit small datasets.

31
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:51.033
The knobs to respect are the number of leaves and the minimum data in a leaf.

32
00:01:52.233 --> 00:01:53.159
Let's recap.

33
00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:56.167
LightGBM is boosting rebuilt for speed.

34
00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:58.367
Histogram bins replace sorting.

35
00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:00.800
Leaf-wise growth makes deeper trees.

36
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.764
Watch the number of leaves on small data.

37
00:02:03.764 --> 00:02:04.933
Next up: CatBoost.
